  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...GOES-19 TPW imagery shows a fairly dry airmass \nacross the region, with PW values around 1.5-1.6 inches. This \ncombined with ridge aloft will keep shower/storm chances low (around \n10-20%), and onshore winds will focus the sea breeze collision west \nof Lake County into this evening. Therefore mostly dry conditions \nare expected to continue through the remainder of the afternoon and \nevening across east central FL. However later this evening and into \nthe overnight hours, deeper moisture associated with an approaching \nweak inverted trough will begin to move into the waters. This will \nlead to an increase in isolated to scattered showers and storms over \nthe adjacent Atlantic waters, especially south of the Cape, with \nsome of this activity reaching and moving onshore along the coast. \nPoPs increase to 20-40% near to south of Melbourne, with greatest \npotential for rain along the Treasure Coast. Remaining warm and \nhumid into tonight, with lows in the mid to upper 70s for most \nlocations. \n\nFriday-Saturday...Deeper moisture (PW values around 2 inches) \noverspreads the area into tomorrow and remains in place through \nSaturday. This will lead to rising rain chances across the region, \nwith PoPs as high as 30-60% each day and greatest shower and storm \ncoverage focused near to south of Orlando. Atlantic ridge axis will \nremain north of the area tomorrow, maintaining an onshore flow and \nsteering scattered showers and storms westward through the \nafternoon, with conditions drying out for the evening. Ridge axis \nwill then begin to slide back southward and across the area Saturday \nas it weakens, with sea breeze collision focused across inland \nportions of east central FL. A few stronger storms each day will be \ncapable of gusty winds, frequent lightning strikes, and locally \nheavy downpours. \n\nDespite the increase in shower/storm coverage, highs will still be \nnear to above normal in the low to mid 90s for much of area. Peak \nheat index values 100-107F will also nudge closer to Heat Advisory \ncriteria (108-110F) for portions of the area on Saturday, so will \nhave to continue to monitor. Either way, widespread Moderate to \nMajor HeatRisk conditions will continue to impact those without \nadequate cooling and hydration. \n\nSunday-Wednesday...(Modified Previous Discussion) Ridge axis shifts \nsouth and remains south of the area from Sunday through the middle \nof next week. Deep moisture (PW of 2+ inches) remains across east \ncentral Florida as a cold front slowly sags across the southeast \nUnited States. Strengthening offshore flow out of the west-southwest \nwill pin the sea breeze closer to the coast, but could be strong \nenough to prevent sea breeze development at times. The deeper \nmoisture and sea breeze collisions favored across the eastern side\nof the peninsula will keep rain chances above normal, with \nscattered to numerous showers and storms each afternoon and \nevening. Outside of showers and storms, high temperatures will\ncontinue to range from the low to mid 90s. Peak heat index values\nwill still easily reach around 102-107F each afternoon, and could\ncontinue to see portions of the region reach Heat Advisory \ncriteria.",
